INT. CONVENT WINE CELLAR - NORTHERN ITALY - NIGHT

Vaulted brick, rows of dusty barrels, a single oil lamp on an upturned crate. Overhead, far away, the dull thump of artillery. Dust sifts from the ceiling with each one.

LIEUTENANT HOLLIS WEBB (30s, American, clean-shaven despite everything, map case at his hip) sits on a barrel, writing in a notebook.

CORPORAL TUCKER MAYES (20s, freckled, helmet pushed back) stands guard at the foot of the stone stairs, rifle slung.

A MAN in a ragged civilian coat sits on the floor in the shadows, hands resting on his knees. He is gaunt, gray at the temples, and watches the lamp rather than the soldiers.

MAYES
Lieutenant, he's still not saying a word.

WEBB
Give him time.

MAYES
Been an hour, sir.

WEBB
Been four years for him.

Another thump. Dust falls. The lamp flame shivers.

MAYES
That one's closer.

WEBB
They're walking it down the valley. It'll pass us by morning.

MAYES
How do you know?

WEBB
Because it's the same gun, the same interval. Count with me. Twenty seconds, then the next one lands a little to the left.

MAYES
You been counting?

WEBB
It's what there is to do.

Mayes shifts his weight. Looks at the stranger, then back.

MAYES
Sir, if he's a spy, he's a bad one. Nobody sneaks in and then sits down to wait.

WEBB
Nobody said spy.

MAYES
Sergeant Pruitt said spy.

WEBB
Sergeant Pruitt says a lot of things.

MAYES
He said it twice.

WEBB
He says everything twice. It's how you know he means it, or doesn't.

The STRANGER lifts his head. His English is careful, formal.

STRANGER
It is the Abbess's wine you are sitting on.

WEBB
We'll try not to drink it.

STRANGER
She would be angry if you did not.

Webb closes the notebook. Mayes straightens, hand drifting toward his rifle, then stops.

WEBB
You speak English.

STRANGER
I taught it. Before. At the school in Cremona.

WEBB
What's your name?

STRANGER
Anselm.

WEBB
Anselm what?

ANSELM
Only Anselm. The rest belongs to a man who no longer exists.

MAYES
That's a hell of a thing to say.

ANSELM
It is a hell of a year.

WEBB
Anselm, we're dug in here until the line moves. Nobody's going to hurt you. We just need to know who else is in this convent.

ANSELM
Nine sisters. Two children from the village. One goat.

MAYES
A goat?

ANSELM
She gives good milk. You will want to thank her.

WEBB
Where are they now?

ANSELM
In the chapel, praying very quietly. They are afraid of your Corporal.

MAYES
Me?

ANSELM
You walk like a man who expects to be shot at.

MAYES
I do expect to be shot at.

ANSELM
Yes. That is why they are afraid.

Webb almost smiles. Another thump, softer this time. No one flinches.

WEBB
That one was farther.

ANSELM
They are walking it down the valley.

WEBB
So I hear.

MAYES
Lieutenant, permission to ask something?

WEBB
Go ahead.

MAYES
Why are we being so polite?

WEBB
Because it's night, and it's cold, and the only war in this room right now is between your stomach and the rations.

MAYES
That's a war I'm losing.

ANSELM
There is bread upstairs. And cheese. The sisters will not offer. You must ask.

WEBB
Mayes, go ask.

MAYES
Sir, my post.

WEBB
I have the post. Go ask nicely.

MAYES
Nicely. Yes, sir.

Mayes starts up the stairs, pauses halfway.

MAYES (CONT'D)
Do I say please in Italian?

ANSELM
Per favore. And you bow your head. Slightly.

MAYES
Per favore.

ANSELM
Less like you are giving an order.

MAYES
Per favore.

ANSELM
Better.

Mayes climbs out of sight. Webb opens the notebook again, then turns it face down on his knee.

WEBB
You must have been a good teacher.

ANSELM
I was a patient one. It is not the same.

WEBB
It's most of it.

The lamp steadies. Above them, a faint scrape of boots on stone, and then a woman's voice, very small, saying something that might be a blessing.
